14
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.402
Why does the scope matter
as much as the policy?

15
00:00:52.402 --> 00:00:56.233
Because Conditional Access for
workload identities is narrow,

16
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:01.003
as Microsoft Learn's Conditional Access
for workload identities page sets out.

17
00:01:01.003 --> 00:01:03.215
Block is the only grant control.

18
00:01:03.215 --> 00:01:08.384
It covers single-tenant service principals
registered in your tenant, and nothing

19
00:01:08.384 --> 00:01:13.403
else: not Microsoft or SaaS apps, not
multitenant apps, and not managed identities.

20
00:01:13.403 --> 00:01:17.977
A policy assigned to a group that holds a
service principal isn't enforced for it;

21
00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.889
you have to assign the
service principal directly.

22
00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:25.278
And Workload I D Premium is needed
to create or change these policies.

23
00:01:25.278 --> 00:01:29.891
So in this module you hold the
program's two policies, C A oh fifteen

24
00:01:29.891 --> 00:01:34.590
for location and C A oh thirty-seven
for high risk, as reviewed files.

25
00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.870
They're scoped by object
I D, one ring at a time,

26
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.970
and every one is created report-only.

27
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.599
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:50.282
which takes about sixty-five
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:50.282 --> 00:01:54.393
You'll state the four limits of
Conditional Access for workload identities,

30
00:01:54.393 --> 00:01:56.807
and what covers the
identities outside them.

31
00:01:56.807 --> 00:01:59.535
You'll explain why each policy is rendered

32
00:01:59.535 --> 00:02:02.419
once per ring and always
created report-only.

33
00:02:02.419 --> 00:02:04.245
You'll run the pre-merge check,

34
00:02:04.245 --> 00:02:07.970
the tenant check and the plan,
and read what each one reports.

35
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:12.532
You'll read a sign-in summary and say
whether a ring is ready for enforcement.

36
00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:15.783
And you'll see a ring held
without the allow-enforce switch,

37
00:02:15.783 --> 00:02:19.033
enforced with it, and held again
when the summary is edited.

38
00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:23.435
Before you start, finish Module 5, the
other half of Gate 4, and Module 1,

39
00:02:23.435 --> 00:02:27.523
because the scope names service
principals by the enterprise

